!!id:"https://schemas.example.com/2026/38/orders/orders-api-1.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/38/io/ltr8/http/meta-service.tn"
!!import:"https://schemas.example.com/2026/38/orders/orders-1.tn"
@doc:"""
  Both: the interface of orders-1.tn, and the api that implements it. Every endpoint is a `!binding`
  naming a method and borrowing its signature. The method is a reference the resolver judges, a qualified
  name through the interface's entry, its key and its methods -- `orders.orders.methods.place_order` -- so a
  misspelt method fails at load. What a binding adds is the placement of that method's one request record
  over the HTTP request, which is where the two views are allowed to look different:

    POST   /orders        new_order   { order  idempotency_key }  order unwrapped as the body,
                                                                  idempotency_key as Idempotency-Key
    GET    /orders/{id}   order_ref   { id }                      id from the path
    GET    /orders        order_query { status  page  page_size } all three in the query (a GET's remainder)
    DELETE /orders/{id}   order_ref   { id }                      id from the path; nothing left over

  `implements: [orders.orders]` is a claim the reader holds this api to: every method of `orders` has a
  binding here, or an entry in `not_bound` giving the reason it does not.
  """
{
  orders_api => !apis { orders_api => {
    implements: [orders.orders]
    resources: {
      "/orders" => !resource {
        @summary:"Place an order."
        @doc:"""
          Accepts a new order and confirms it. Repeating the request with the same Idempotency-Key returns the
          original confirmation rather than placing a second order.
          """
        POST => !binding { method: orders.orders.methods.place_order  status: 201
                           body: order  headers: { idempotency_key => "Idempotency-Key" } }
        @summary:"Search orders."
        GET  => !binding { method: orders.orders.methods.list_orders }
      }
      "/orders/{id}" => !resource {
        GET    => !binding { method: orders.orders.methods.get_order }
        DELETE => !binding { method: orders.orders.methods.cancel_order  status: 204 }
      }
    }
  } }
}
